1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,000
Power cuts are an ordinary night in Ukraine.
Will the battery last until sunrise?

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:10,200
A five kilowatt roof in Kyiv. Battery size, charge, and household load
are typed, not read from a meter.

3
00:00:10,200 --> 00:00:13,200
The browser signs in with OAuth, the same shape Alexa+ asks for.

4
00:00:13,200 --> 00:00:14,600
Sol checks the forecast before it answers.

5
00:00:14,600 --> 00:00:20,600
Night battery check. Yes. That charge should last until sunrise,
and this dim day does not refill it.

6
00:00:20,600 --> 00:00:26,300
The washing machine waits until tomorrow, from 1 to 3 in the afternoon,
when the roof can cover it.

7
00:00:26,300 --> 00:00:32,500
Day plan. The washer fits that window.
The dishwasher and the car charging do not.

8
00:00:32,500 --> 00:00:44,300
Morning briefing. The strongest stretch today is 1 to 4 in the afternoon,
and it is too thin for heavy loads.

9
00:00:44,300 --> 00:00:49,400
The trace under the page is the MCP conversation.
Protocol 2025-11-25, over Streamable HTTP.

10
00:00:49,600 --> 00:00:57,700
Asked plainly, the roof is about 1.3 kilowatt-hours today,
with a brighter day tomorrow.

11
00:00:58,200 --> 00:01:04,200
The official client lists the tools and agrees on protocol 2025-11-25.

12
00:01:04,400 --> 00:01:10,200
A live call from that client answers the washing machine in one sentence.

13
00:01:10,400 --> 00:01:15,000
Eighteen tests pass, and none of them call the live forecast.

14
00:01:15,200 --> 00:01:31,040
The project is public on GitHub. Open-Meteo and PVGIS.
Built by Vadym Bilobrovets.
